{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q003_actin_like_atpase_divergence","answer":"In SCOPe 2.08 the three catalytic domains share one superfamily, c.55.1 "Actin-like ATPase domain" (sunid 53067). Its lineage is class c (alpha and beta proteins, a/b), fold c.55 (Ribonuclease H-like motif), then superfamily c.55.1. They sit in different families below it: Actin/HSP70 is c.55.1.1 (sunid 53068), Hexokinase is c.55.1.3 (sunid 53083), and Glycerol kinase is c.55.1.4 (sunid 53089). I confirmed the lineages of two hexokinase and glycerol kinase reference domains, d4foea1 (hexokinase, c.55.1.3) and d1r59o1 (glycerol kinase, c.55.1.4). I did not retrieve a domain from the Actin/HSP70 family. Its family placement comes from the superfamily's child listing. The superfamily's homology response has homology_implied_by_level=true, homology_scope sf, and no warnings.

What this permits: because they share a superfamily but not a family, SCOPe asserts probable common ancestry (a remote homology) that is more distant than family-level relatedness. It is supported by structure and conserved features, not by close sequence similarity. You can reasonably infer a shared ancestral actin-like ATPase core fold. This supports using the superfamily as a framework for structural comparison and for hypotheses about conserved nucleotide-binding architecture. It also fits the superfamily annotation "duplication contains two domains of this fold", which describes a two-lobed arrangement, though I did not inspect any structure to verify it.

What it does not permit: the shared superfamily does not show that the three proteins have the same substrate, reaction, or mechanism. Actin/HSP70, hexokinase and glycerol kinase have clearly diverged in function. Sharing a superfamily does not make one a reliable template for another's active site, regulation or ligand specificity. Sequence-identity transfer, family-level function transfer, and the choice of an interchangeable structural template are all unsupported at this level. It also does not make them family-level relatives. The fold c.55 level alone would only indicate structural similarity, but here the superfamily level carries the homology claim. I did not retrieve alignments or literature, so I make no claim about the evolutionary history beyond what the hierarchy asserts.","evidence_used":["mcp__scope__search_scop","mcp__scope__get_scop_node","mcp__scope__list_scop_children","mcp__scope__get_scop_domains","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/annotations","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/homology","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sunids/53067/children?limit=30","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d4foea1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/sids/d1r59o1/parents","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=hexokinase&mode=auto&limit=10","/api/v1/releases/2.08/search?q=glycerol+kinase&mode=auto&limit=10","scop-homology/SKILL.md"]}]}
